+# AWS — Private Endpoint & BYOK Runbook for LLM Vendors
+
+This runbook outlines the typical steps to enable vendor private endpoints (PrivateLink) and BYOK workflows for LLM providers on AWS. Work with vendor support — details vary by vendor.
+
+1) Procurement & Vendor Contact
+- Request vendor private endpoint support (AWS PrivateLink / Private Service Connect) and BYOK onboarding docs. Obtain account-specific instructions and vendor contact.
+
+2) Network: Configure VPC Endpoint
+- Create a VPC Endpoint Service connection to vendor's service endpoint (if vendor exposes service).
+- If vendor provides a service name, create a VPC Endpoint in your VPC:
+  - aws ec2 create-vpc-endpoint --vpc-id <vpc> --service-name <com.amazonaws.vpce.<id>> --vpc-endpoint-type Interface --subnet-ids <subnet-ids> --security-group-ids <sg>
+- Ensure security groups allow egress from k8s worker nodes to endpoint; restrict egress to vendor endpoint only.
+
+3) DNS & Routing
+- Configure private DNS (or Route53 private hosted zone) so the vendor hostname (e.g., api.anthropic.internal) resolves to the private endpoint.
+- Update k8s pods with DNS policy or use kube-dns config.
+
+4) BYOK (Customer-Managed Key)
+- Create a customer-managed KMS key in AWS (KMS) with appropriate key policy that allows vendor or required operations only if vendor supports key import.
+- If vendor supports importing public key material: follow their BYOK import procedure. Typically:
+  - Generate key material / CSR per vendor spec.
+  - Upload to vendor portal; verify vendor acknowledges import.
+  - Vendor returns key id / binding.
+- If vendor supports envelope encryption or EKM integration, configure the vendor to use your KMS as EKM endpoint (if supported).
+
+5) IAM & Secrets
+- Create IAM role/service account for adapter to access private endpoint and rotate keys.
+- Store vendor API keys in Vault; use Vault Agent Injector or CSI Secrets Store to project into pods.
+
+6) Validation & Testing
+- From a pod with access, curl the private endpoint; ensure calls do not route to public internet via egress checks (VPC Flow Logs or temporary firewall deny).
+- Validate that the vendor acknowledges your BYOK key usage and that no secret material is retained contrary to the DPA.
+
+7) Observability & Guardrails
+- Add network flow logs and monitor unusual egress.
+- Implement budget guard in adapter to avoid runaway costs.
+- Add playbook to revoke or rotate keys quickly.
+
+Vendor notes:
+- Not all vendors offer BYOK or private endpoints; if unavailable, employ local redaction/RAG and on‑prem model fallback.
+
