+# GCP — Private Service Connect & BYOK Runbook for LLM Vendors
+
+This runbook covers steps to enable Private Service Connect (PSC) / Workload Identity and BYOK for vendors on GCP.
+
+1) Procurement & Vendor Contact
+- Request vendor instructions for PSC endpoint and BYOK with Vertex/Cloud KMS as applicable.
+
+2) Private Service Connect (PSC)
+- Configure a PSC endpoint towards the vendor's PSC service (the vendor will provide the target service).
+- Create a PSC Forwarding Rule / attachment in your VPC. Use internal ingress rules to route vendor hostname to PSC.
+- Ensure DNS resolves the vendor host to the PSC private IP (Cloud DNS private zone).
+
+3) Workload Identity & IAM
+- Use Workload Identity for GKE pods to access Cloud resources and avoid long-lived service account keys.
+- Create a service account for the adapter and grant necessary IAM roles (compute.networkUser, secretmanager.secretAccessor etc).
+
+4) BYOK with Cloud KMS
+- If vendor supports BYOK with Cloud KMS, create a key ring and a key, and follow vendor import steps.
+- Some vendors accept public key or wrap keys; others use KMS-as-a-service integrations.
+
+5) Secrets & Secret Manager
+- Store vendor credentials in Secret Manager or Vault. Use Workload Identity to grant read access to adapter pods.
+
+6) Validation & Testing
+- Test connectivity from GKE pod using `curl` to PSC endpoint; verify traffic via VPC Flow Logs.
+- Confirm vendor indicates key association / usage per BYOK flow.
+
+7) Operational Controls
+- Alert on egress to any public endpoint for vendor hostnames.
+- Periodically audit key usage and retention per DPA.
+
